      <item>
        <title>Willow 2: 105-qubit surface code operating below threshold — first logical qubit with net error reduction</title>
        <link>https://blog.google/quantum/willow-2</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://blog.google/quantum/willow-2</guid>
        <pubDate>Fri, 15 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Google Research blog</source>
        <description>Google&#x27;s Willow 2 is a 105-qubit superconducting processor (transmon) that demonstrates the surface code operating below the error-correction threshold. The key milestone: a distance-5 surface code (25 data qubits + 24 ancilla qubits) achieves a logical error rate of 0.0003 per cycle — below the physical error rate of 0.001, proving that adding more qubits reduces the logical error (exponential suppression). This is the first experimental demonstration that quantum error correction works as theory predicts — the &#x27;below threshold&#x27; regime. Distance-3 → distance-5 shows the expected exponential improvement. Extrapolation to distance-7 (49 data qubits) predicts 0.0001 logical error — sufficient for fault-tolerant quantum computing. Willow 2 is available via Google Quantum Cloud. 67 research groups have access. The system runs the surface code at 1 MHz cycle rate (1µs per syndrome-extraction round).</description>
        <category>Physics - Quantum error correction</category>
        <dc:creator>Google Quantum AI</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Genie 3: 100B-param generative physics world model — interactive 3D simulations from text</title>
        <link>https://deepmind.com/genie-3</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://deepmind.com/genie-3</guid>
        <pubDate>Sun, 17 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>DeepMind blog</source>
        <description>Genie 3 is DeepMind&#x27;s 100B-parameter (MoE 128 experts, top-2 routing) generative world model. The 2027 update adds full-physics simulation: fluid dynamics, rigid-body collision, electromagnetism, and thermodynamics — all generated from a text prompt and running at 60 FPS interactive on an H100. On the PhysBench benchmark (50 physics scenarios × 5 modalities), Genie 3 achieves 0.92 correlation with traditional physics solvers (OpenFOAM, PyBullet, COMSOL) at 10000x speedup. Users can perturb the simulation in real-time (move objects, change gravity, add forces) and the world model adapts instantly. Released as Genie 3 API (cloud) + Genie 3 Edge (12B variant, runs at 30 FPS on RTX 4090). 47 universities use it for physics education + research. The model generates worlds that are physically consistent — conservation laws, energy, momentum all hold within 2%.</description>
        <category>CS - Generative physics worlds</category>
        <dc:creator>DeepMind Genie team</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Claude 5 Opus: 1T-param reasoning model with 1M-token thinking — solves 3 IMO gold problems</title>
        <link>https://anthropic.com/claude-5-opus</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://anthropic.com/claude-5-opus</guid>
        <pubDate>Mon, 18 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Anthropic blog</source>
        <description>Claude 5 Opus is Anthropic&#x27;s 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 512 experts, top-8 routing) reasoning model. The &#x27;Opus&#x27; variant extends Claude 5&#x27;s thinking budget from 100k to 1 million tokens, enabling multi-hour deductive reasoning. Active params: ~80B per token (top-8 of 1T). On the International Mathematical Olympiad (IMO) 2027 benchmark, Claude 5 Opus solves 3 of 6 problems at gold-medal level (geometry, number theory, combinatorics). This is the first AI to solve IMO problems without hints — the previous best (o3-pro science) solved 1 of 6. On ARC-AGI-2, Claude 5 Opus scores 0.83 (beating Claude 5 0.71, o3-pro 0.62). On the METR agent benchmark (long-horizon tasks), it completes 47% of 100-step tasks (vs Claude 5&#x27;s 22%). Released via Anthropic API; cost: $50 per 1M thinking tokens.</description>
        <category>CS - Constitutional reasoning</category>
        <dc:creator>Anthropic Research</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>ESM-6: 500B-param generative protein model — 68% first-try foldable + enzyme design</title>
        <link>https://ai.meta.com/esm-6</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://ai.meta.com/esm-6</guid>
        <pubDate>Sat, 16 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Meta AI blog</source>
        <description>ESM-6 is Meta&#x27;s 500B-parameter (MoE 128 experts, top-4 routing) generative protein design + function model. The v6 release adds an enzyme-design head that produces novel enzymes with specified catalytic activity — 23 of 50 designed enzymes show measurable catalytic activity in vitro (vs ESM-5&#x27;s 12/50). On the ProteinGym benchmark (47 design tasks), ESM-6 achieves 0.88 designability (vs ESM-5 0.82, RFdiffusion 0.68). First-try foldable rate: 68% (vs ESM-5 52%, ESM-4 38%, ESM-3 18% — the curve is approaching saturation). Key innovation: a multi-modal protein tokenizer that encodes sequence, structure, and function in a unified latent space. This allows cross-modal generation: given a function, generate a sequence; given a structure, generate a function; given a sequence, predict structure+function.</description>
        <category>Biology - Generative protein FM</category>
        <dc:creator>Meta AI FAIR + EvolutionaryScale</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Quantinuum Helios: 10,000-qubit trapped-ion quantum computer with fault-tolerant operation</title>
        <link>https://quantinuum.com/helios-2027</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://quantinuum.com/helios-2027</guid>
        <pubDate>Thu, 14 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Quantinuum blog</source>
        <description>Quantinuum Helios is a 10,000-qubit trapped-ion quantum computer (Ytterbium-171 + Barium-138 dual-species). The dual-species architecture enables native mid-circuit measurement + feedforward — the first hardware to support real-time quantum error correction without slow optical readout. Helios runs a distance-7 surface code (49 data + 48 ancilla = 97 qubits per logical qubit) at 0.0001 logical error rate — fault-tolerant threshold. The remaining 9,903 qubits support 102 logical qubits — sufficient for Shor&#x27;s algorithm on RSA-2048 (estimated 20M logical-qubit-cycles, ~4 hours on Helios). Available via Quantinuum Cloud. 120 university + 47 industry research groups. The system&#x27;s fully-connected topology + 0.005% two-qubit gate error + mid-circuit measurement make it the leading platform for fault-tolerant quantum computing.</description>
        <category>Physics - Large-scale trapped-ion QC</category>
        <dc:creator>Quantinuum</dc:creator>
      </item>
